<BaseLayout title="Linear Algebra" description="The language of states, bases, operators, and spectra in electronic structure." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Linear Algebra</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Linear Algebra</h1>
  <p class="lede">Electronic-structure theory repeatedly turns physical objects into vectors, operators into matrices, and stationary states into eigenvalue problems. Linear algebra is the language that makes those translations precise.</p>
  <p>This page is not a complete linear-algebra course. It isolates the ideas that recur when quantum states are represented in a finite basis and when electronic-structure equations are solved numerically.</p>

  <h2>Representing a state</h2>
  <p>A quantum state is not a column of numbers by itself. The numbers are coordinates relative to a chosen basis. In a finite representation, a state can be written as</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="psi ket equals the sum from i equals one to N of c sub i times phi sub i ket">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <munderover>
            <mo>∑</mo>
            <mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
            <mi>N</mi>
          </munderover>
          <msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub>
          <mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo>
          <mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\psi\rangle=\sum_&#123;i=1&#125;^&#123;N&#125;c_i|\phi_i\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The basis functions <math class="math-inline" aria-label="phi sub i ket"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\phi_i\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> specify the representation; the coefficients <math class="math-inline" aria-label="c sub i"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_i</annotation></semantics></math> specify the state within that representation. Changing the basis changes the coordinates, but it should not change the underlying physical state when both representations are sufficiently complete.</p>
  <p>This distinction is fundamental for plane waves, localized atomic orbitals, Gaussian functions, finite elements, and real-space grids. A basis is a numerical choice, not a new physical theory.</p>

  <h2>Inner products give geometry</h2>
  <p>Inner products define lengths, normalization, angles, and orthogonality. They allow statements such as “these two states are orthogonal” or “this trial state is normalized” to have a precise meaning.</p>
  <p>In an orthonormal basis, the coefficient-space inner product has the familiar form <math class="math-inline" aria-label="c dagger d"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;c&#125;^&#123;\dagger&#125;\mathbf&#123;d&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. In a non-orthogonal basis, the overlap matrix enters:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="inner product of psi and chi equals c dagger S d, with S sub i j equal to inner product of phi sub i and phi sub j">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" rowspacing="0.65em">
          <mtr>
            <mtd><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mtd>
            <mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">d</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd>
          </mtr>
          <mtr>
            <mtd><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd>
            <mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd>
            <mtd><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd>
          </mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin&#123;aligned&#125;\langle\psi|\chi\rangle&amp;=\mathbf&#123;c&#125;^&#123;\dagger&#125;\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\mathbf&#123;d&#125;,\\S_&#123;ij&#125;&amp;=\langle\phi_i|\phi_j\rangle.\end&#123;aligned&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The matrix <math class="math-inline" aria-label="S"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;S&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore not an optional correction. It records the geometry induced by the chosen basis. Near-linear dependence among basis functions makes the overlap matrix ill-conditioned and can make otherwise reasonable calculations numerically fragile.</p>

  <h2>Operators become spectral problems</h2>
  <p>A linear operator maps one state to another. Observables and Hamiltonians are represented by Hermitian operators, whose finite matrices have real eigenvalues and mutually orthogonal eigenspaces under the relevant inner product.</p>
  <p>For an orthonormal basis, the stationary equation becomes</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="H c equals epsilon c">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">H</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;H&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;=\varepsilon\mathbf&#123;c&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>For a non-orthogonal basis, the same physical problem becomes a generalized eigenvalue problem:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="H c equals epsilon S c">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">H</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;H&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;=\varepsilon\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>For linearly independent basis functions, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="S is Hermitian positive definite"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mtext> is Hermitian positive definite</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\text&#123; is Hermitian positive definite&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. The generalized eigenvectors can then be normalized in the overlap metric:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="c m dagger S c n equals delta m n">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;c&#125;_m^&#123;\dagger&#125;\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;_n=\delta_&#123;mn&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This is the algebraic core behind many orbital calculations. The matrix elements, the overlap matrix, the dimension of the represented subspace, and the algorithm used to solve the problem all affect the numerical result.</p>

  <h2>Subspaces matter more than individual vectors</h2>
  <p>Many electronic-structure quantities depend on an occupied subspace rather than on a unique set of orbitals. Unitary rotations within a fully occupied subspace can change the individual orbital shapes without changing the density matrix or total occupied space.</p>
  <p>This is why degeneracy, symmetry-adapted blocks, projector methods, density matrices, and Wannier transformations are naturally expressed in terms of subspaces. Treating every displayed orbital as a uniquely defined physical object can therefore be misleading.</p>

  <h2>Variational reasoning in matrix form</h2>
  <p>The Rayleigh quotient connects eigenvalues to minimization:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="R of c equals c dagger H c divided by c dagger S c">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>R</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac>
            <mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">H</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi></mrow>
            <mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">S</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">c</mi></mrow>
          </mfrac>
          <mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\mathbf&#123;c&#125;)=\frac&#123;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;^&#123;\dagger&#125;\mathbf&#123;H&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;&#125;&#123;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;^&#123;\dagger&#125;\mathbf&#123;S&#125;\mathbf&#123;c&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Within a chosen subspace, minimizing this quotient gives the lowest represented eigenvalue. Enlarging a well-constructed variational basis can improve the approximation, but the conclusion depends on the method and representation. A small residual or a stable eigenvalue within one basis does not by itself establish basis completeness.</p>

  <h2>What becomes numerical</h2>
  <p>Electronic-structure calculations rarely use dense matrices of modest size. They exploit sparsity, symmetry, matrix-vector products, low-rank structure, and iterative extraction of only part of a spectrum. Several ideas are therefore especially valuable beyond the introductory course:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Singular-value decomposition</strong> for rank, conditioning, compression, and near-linear dependence.</li>
    <li><strong>Generalized Hermitian eigenproblems</strong> for non-orthogonal bases.</li>
    <li><strong>Projection and orthogonalization</strong> for constrained subspaces and iterative solvers.</li>
    <li><strong>Sparse and iterative methods</strong> when forming or diagonalizing the full matrix is impractical.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The error and stability questions associated with these algorithms belong to <a href={href('/theory/numerical-analysis/')}>Numerical Analysis</a>.</p>

  <h2>How to study this subject</h2>
  <p><a href="https://linear.axler.net/">Sheldon Axler, <cite>Linear Algebra Done Right</cite>, 4th ed.</a> is a rigorous route through linear maps, invariant subspaces, eigenvalues, and spectral structure. The author provides a legal open version under a noncommercial license. It is not a numerical-algorithms text.</p>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-335j-introduction-to-numerical-methods-spring-2019/">MIT OpenCourseWare 18.335J, Introduction to Numerical Methods</a> is an advanced continuation for conditioning, QR, SVD, iterative methods, and eigenvalue algorithms. It assumes substantial linear algebra and programming maturity.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.icourse163.org/course/gucas-1207039810">University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, <cite>Linear Algebra</cite></a> is a Chinese-language undergraduate video course led by the course team around Xi Nanhua. It gives a low-barrier route through the basic algebraic objects and methods before the abstract language used in this page becomes natural. It is appropriate for a first pass after secondary-school mathematics; its introductory scope does not replace proof-level spectral theory or numerical linear-algebra practice.</p>
  <p><a href="https://hefferon.net/linearalgebra/">Jim Hefferon, <cite>Linear Algebra</cite></a> is a freely available author-maintained text that moves from systems and elimination to vector spaces, maps, determinants, and eigenvalues with many worked computational transitions. It is useful when matrix manipulation and the coordinate-free viewpoint still feel disconnected. Readers need basic calculus; it does not replace the conditioning, sparse algorithms, or generalized eigenproblems used in large electronic-structure calculations.</p>

  <h2>Minimum recognition target</h2>
  <p>Before moving into quantum mechanics or basis-set methods, a learner should be able to distinguish a state from its coordinates, explain what an inner product contributes, recognize Hermitian and unitary structure, interpret ordinary and generalized eigenvalue equations, and identify when a basis or overlap matrix may be poorly conditioned.</p>
</BaseLayout>
